.TH IOURING_MONITOR 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iouring_monitor \- record io_uring read/write intent and completions with eBPF
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iouring_monitor
.RB [ \-\-pid " PID" ]
.RB [ \-\-dur " SECONDS" ]
.RB [ \-\-jsonl " PATH" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iouring_monitor
attaches to accepted io_uring read/write preparation paths and the completion
tracepoint.  It records intent and completion events for tracked requests; it
does not trace
.BR IORING_OP_URING_CMD .
It can restrict process-scoped events to one process and writes a final drop
record so consumers can reject an incomplete capture.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
Restrict the capture to one thread-group ID.
.TP
.BI \-\-dur " seconds"
Stop after the given duration.  Otherwise stop with SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.TP
.BI \-\-jsonl " path"
Write newline-delimited JSON to the path instead of standard output.
.SH FIDELITY
This observes io_uring, not system-call counts and not the NVMe device.  An
application writes SQEs into shared memory.  One
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
can submit many SQEs, and SQPOLL can consume SQEs without a system call per IO.
This monitor observes supported read/write events, whereas
.BR syscall_monitor (1)
does not hook
.BR io_uring_enter (2)
or see those requests.
.PP
Filesystems and the block layer can split, merge, cache, or reorder requests
below this point.  Use
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
when the device command stream is the required witness.
.SH PRIVACY
The trace contains process and request metadata.  It does not copy IO payloads,
but identifiers, lengths, and timing can still reveal workload structure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kvio (1),
.BR syscall_monitor (1),
.BR nvme_uring_cmd_monitor (1),
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
